schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Bitcoin's proof-of-work energy use: ~138 TWh/year (Cambridge, 2025)"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Bitcoin secures itself via proof of work "using on the order of 138
    terawatt-hours a year (Cambridge, 2025), comparable to a mid-sized
    country."
  evidence: >-
    Attributed to the Cambridge Bitcoin Electricity Consumption Index, an
    independent, regularly updated estimate maintained by the University of
    Cambridge, linked in the article's references.
  source_lineage: "src/content/docs/what-is-blockchain.md — Cambridge Bitcoin Electricity Consumption Index (https://ccaf.io/cbnsi/cbeci)"
  interpretation: >-
    Used to ground the "energy" honesty section — contrasted directly with
    Ethereum's post-Merge proof-of-stake energy use to show the claim
    "blockchain wastes energy" does not generalize across consensus
    mechanisms.
  uncertainty: >-
    Cambridge's index is a continuously updated estimate, not a fixed
    figure — "on the order of 138 TWh" should be treated as a snapshot
    (dated 2025 per the article) that will drift over time; verify against
    the live index before citing a precise number.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-blockchain.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
